[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! Differential test harness: C `libdriver.so` vs Rust `libdriver.so`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! Both libraries are loaded with `libloading` and called **only** through
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! their exported symbols, exactly as an external consumer would — the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are therefore under test too. Rust functions are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! never called directly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! # Why this harness is shaped the way it is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! `bad()` reproduces the C's CWE-457 defect: it forwards an *uninitialized*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! `char *` to `printLine`, so the bytes that reach stdout are raw stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! residue. A naive comparison therefore measures the harness rather than the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! library. Two artifacts must be eliminated (see CONFIGS.md):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! 1. **argv/env contents.** The stale pointer aims into the top-of-stack
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    `argv`/`envp` string area. The two libraries' natural paths differ in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    length, which shifts that area and changes the printed bytes. Both `.so`s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    are therefore copied to **equal-length paths**, and both children are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    spawned with **identical argv and an identical environment**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! 2. **ASLR.** When the residue *is* a stack address, its bytes are the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    randomized address itself, so two processes disagree by construction.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    Randomization is disabled in the child via `personality(ADDR_NO_RANDOMIZE)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!    before `exec`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! Each case runs one child process per library. The child redirects fd 1 to a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! file, runs an op program, flushes C stdio, and `_exit`s, so the captured
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! bytes are exactly the library's output with no test-harness chatter.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //!
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! `RTLD_LAZY` and `RTLD_NOW` are both exercised: the C `.so` uses lazy PLT
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! binding, so the first intra-library call runs `_dl_runtime_resolve`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! overwrites the very stack word `bad()` reads. That is observable, so it is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m //! configuration axis rather than an implementation detail.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m use std::ffi::{c_char, c_int, c_void, OsStr};
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m use std::io::Write;
