[1mUnprimed direct [38;5;10mbad()[0m calls are not matchable by any translation, and I proved it rather than assumed it.[22m [38;5;10mbad()[0m forwards whatever occupies its [38;5;10m-0x8(%rbp)[0m slot. When reached through [38;5;10mdriver[0m, that residue comes from library code and matches exactly. When called directly, the last writer is [38;5;10mdlopen[0m, so the value depends on the object's load footprint. The control compares two [3mbehaviourally identical C builds[23m — the shipped source vs. the same source plus 2000 unused exports and one extra [38;5;10mDT_NEEDED[0m — and they disagree on 6 of 6 direct-[38;5;10mbad()[0m rows while agreeing on all 10 library-residue rows. A recompilation of the C already changes the bytes, so Rust cannot be expected to reproduce them. Those rows are compared modulo the residue lines (line count and all other bytes still required to match), and [38;5;10mbad()[0m's actual slot offset is pinned exactly by trampoline rows where the previous [3mlibrary[23m call primes the slot: [38;5;10mgood(); bad()[0m → [38;5;10mstring\nstring\n[0m, [38;5;10mprintLine(NULL); bad()[0m → zero bytes.[0m[0m
